\subsubsection{Simulation Settings}
\label{sec:exp_settings}
\paragraph{Training Set}
The training set contains $10,000$ question-answer pairs. Each question contains $3$ words and each answer $1$ word.

\paragraph{Testing Set}
We evaluate the models on two versions of the test set. One version contains $1,000$ question-answer pairs from the Wikipedia corpus, and the other contains $500$ pairs from the ARQ corpora.

\subsection{Task}

A text simplification model must convert a text $x$ to a simplified text $y$. 

\subsection{Related Tasks}

A text simplification model could be used in combination with a machine translation model to automatically translate a document into another language. This is useful because existing machine translation models often require a large amount of parallel text, which is usually unavailable for low resource languages. 

A text simplification model could also be used to summarize a text, as long as the input text $x$ is long.

  For the sample input sentence, a suitable answer would be: 'the only military capital ship in the fleet survived the attack.'

\section{Data Format}
\label{sec:dataformat}

\subsection{Input}
\label{subsec:input}

The input text is expected in standard \LaTeX{} format. All sections, subsections, figures, tables, \texttt{input}, \texttt{include} and \texttt{usepackage} commands must be removed. The text should appear as one continuous paragraph.

\subsection{Output}

\paragraph{Source: \citet{yin:2019}}
\vspace{2mm}

\noindent The task is to simplify the text as much as possible while maintaining the meaning intact. The difficulty lies in correctly understanding the source text, and knowing which words to change. The reference sentences are created by humans, which can be quite hard to match due to the diversity of human's writing style. We use the same dataset, the same splits, and the same evaluation metric as \citet{yin:2019}.

